---
title: SQL sleuthing with Database Detective
blurb: One of my new favorite learning games; the perfect mix of practical education, mechanical fun, and coziness.
date: 2026-11-02
tags: [misc]
---

<img
	src="/games/database-detective/header.webp"
	alt="Database Detective: Minor Crimes Division title art, a cartoon pig cop peeking over a retro desktop window"
	width="616"
	height="353"
	class="post-screenshot"
/>

I've been playing *Database Detective: Minor Crimes Division* lately, and it's taken
up a frankly embarrassing amount of my evenings. You play a new hire at the Los
Zorangeles police department, and your whole job is solving petty crimes by querying
the city's databases. That's it. You read a case, open the search window, and write
SQL until the answer falls out.

I'll be honest, the learning curve is a little steep. The game doesn't hold your hand
for long, and there were a few cases where I stared at a blank query window for an
uncomfortably long time. But it's careful about *when* it teaches you things. A new
piece of SQL only shows up in the manual once you've hit a case that clearly needs it,
so a `JOIN` arrives right when you've been stuck cross-referencing two tables by hand
and are begging for a better way. After that, you're on your own. There are no hints
waiting to bail you out, just the handful of tools you've been given and a puzzle that
expects you to combine them in ways the manual never spelled out. Struggling through
that and landing on a query that works is genuinely satisfying.

<img
	src="/games/database-detective/manual.webp"
	alt="The in-game desktop, showing a parody SQL for Database Detectives manual, a search window reading SELECT * FROM uhh, and a small pig cop offering help"
	width="1280"
	height="720"
	loading="lazy"
	class="post-screenshot"
/>

The tone is the other half of the fun. Everything looks like a chunky old desktop
with a hand-drawn pig cop who pops up to ask if you need help with SQL, the case, or
your personal problems. It's adorable. It's also, the further you go, pretty messed up.
The "minor crimes" you're solving are often just people saying slightly the wrong
thing online, and the internet in this world is very obviously built for the people
watching it. The game never stops being cute about it, which somehow makes the whole
thing creepier.

What sticks with me most is how well it nails the educational part. A lot of learning
games hand you a skill and then a box shaped exactly like that skill. This one hands
you a pile of skills and a messy problem, and the actual work is figuring out which
ones apply and how to fit them together *before* you type anything. That's what using
SQL (or anything, really) is like in practice, and I'd love to see more games about
learning hold themselves to that standard.
